QUESTIONS.push(
/* ======================= STEREOCHEMISTRY ======================= */
{id:'st-1', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'rs', concept:'chiral-carbon-definition',
 tags:['definition','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What makes a carbon a chiral centre?',
 options:[
  {t:'Four different groups are attached to it', correct:true,
   why:'Four different substituents make the mirror image non-superimposable on the original, which is what chirality means. If any two of the four match, a mirror plane exists and the molecule is identical to its reflection.'},
  {t:'It carries at least one double bond', correct:false,
   why:'A doubly bonded carbon has only three attached groups, so it cannot carry four different ones. Double bonds produce a different kind of stereoisomerism, the cis and trans or E and Z geometry on slides 22 to 24.'},
  {t:'It is bonded to a heteroatom', correct:false,
   why:'A heteroatom is neither required nor sufficient. What decides the question is that all four substituents differ, and a carbon bonded only to carbon and hydrogen chains can qualify.'},
  {t:'It sits in a ring system', correct:false,
   why:'Ring carbons can be chiral, but only under the same four-different-groups test. Rings do independently allow cis and trans isomerism, which is why 1,2-dichlorocyclohexane appears among the diastereomers on slide 26.'}],
 teach:'Chirality is a property of the arrangement rather than of any particular atom, so the test is always the same: are all four groups different? In drugs the centre is usually carbon, but it need not be, and two exceptions are named: esomeprazole has a chiral sulfur and cyclophosphamide a chiral phosphorus. Recognising a centre is the first step of every question in this topic, because ranking the four groups and reading the rotation both depend on having identified one. A molecule with more than one such centre opens the diastereomer possibilities on slide 21.',
 teachImg:'st_chiral_centre',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 7–8',
 quote:'Chiral carbon: A chiral carbon is a carbon in which 4 different groups are attached.'},

{id:'st-2', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'rs', concept:'cip-priority-atomic-number',
 tags:['reasoning','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'How is priority assigned to the groups around a chiral centre?',
 options:[
  {t:'By atomic number, with the highest atomic number getting highest priority', correct:true,
   why:'Priority is decided at the first point of difference. If the atoms bonded directly to the centre differ, atomic number settles it there; if two of them are the same element, the comparison moves outward to the atoms attached to those.'},
  {t:'By the size of the whole substituent group', correct:false,
   why:'Bulk is not the criterion, and following it gives wrong answers regularly. A single bromine outranks a long hydrocarbon chain because bromine has the higher atomic number, despite being far smaller.'},
  {t:'By the number of hydrogens on each group', correct:false,
   why:'Hydrogen count is not the rule. Hydrogen itself is always lowest priority, since it has the lowest atomic number, and that fact is used at the next step to orient the molecule.'},
  {t:'Alphabetically by substituent name', correct:false,
   why:'Naming order plays no part in the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog system, which was built on atomic number precisely so that the answer does not depend on what a group is called.'}],
 teach:'Two rules cover the ranking and they are applied in order. First, the atom with the highest atomic number takes highest priority. Second, when two atoms attached to the centre are the same element, the decision moves to the atoms attached to them and atomic number is compared again, continuing outward until a difference appears. Only after all four are ranked does the third rule apply, which reads the rotation. Keeping the ranking step separate from the reading step is what prevents most errors here, because an error in ranking inverts the final assignment just as surely as an error in orientation does.',
 teachImg:'st_cip_rules',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 9–12',
 quote:'1. Highest priority is given to the atom with the highest atomic number · 2. If two atoms with the same atomic number attached to the chiral center—the priority is given to the next atoms atomic number'},

{id:'st-3', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'rs', concept:'rs-rotation-direction',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'After ranking the four groups, how do you decide between R and S?',
 options:[
  {t:'Put the lowest priority group away from you, then read 1 to 3: clockwise is R, counterclockwise is S', correct:true,
   why:'The rule is stated with the molecule viewed from the side opposite the lowest-priority atom, so the orientation is part of the rule rather than an extra step. From that viewpoint, clockwise gives R and counterclockwise gives S.'},
  {t:'Read 1 to 3 with the lowest priority group pointing toward you: clockwise is R', correct:false,
   why:'The rotation is read correctly but from the wrong side, which inverts the answer. Clockwise with the lowest group toward you means S, and this is the most common way to arrive at exactly the wrong letter.'},
  {t:'Clockwise is always S regardless of orientation', correct:false,
   why:'Two separate mistakes are packed into one answer. The first is the letter: read from the correct side, clockwise gives R, not S. The second is \u2018regardless of orientation\u2019, which cannot hold, because the same centre reads clockwise viewed from one side and counterclockwise viewed from the other. Which direction you see depends entirely on where you are standing, so a rule that drops the viewpoint returns a different configuration for one molecule depending only on how it happens to be drawn.'},
  {t:'R corresponds to dextrorotatory and S to levorotatory', correct:false,
   why:'Absolute configuration and optical rotation are established separately, one from structure and one by measurement. A compound can be R with either sign of rotation, which is why the deck writes the four possibilities as R[+], R[-], S[+] and S[-].'}],
 teach:'The assignment has two independent places to go wrong, and checking them separately is faster than redrawing. The ranking can be wrong, which inverts the answer. The viewpoint can be wrong, which also inverts it, and two such errors would cancel and produce the right letter for the wrong reason. When the lowest-priority group is drawn toward the viewer, the reading can simply be reversed rather than the structure rebuilt. What the letter does not tell you is which way the compound rotates polarized light, since that is measured rather than derived.',
 teachImg:'st_rs_worked',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 10, 13',
 quote:'If first three priorities rotate clockwise assign it as R, if they rotate counterclockwise assign, it as S. The molecule is viewed from the side opposite the lowest-priority atom.'},

{id:'st-4', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'terms', concept:'config-vs-rotation',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Does knowing a compound is R tell you which way it rotates plane-polarized light?',
 options:[
  {t:'No — absolute configuration and optical rotation are determined independently', correct:true,
   why:'R and S are derived from structure by applying the priority rules. Dextrorotatory and levorotatory are measured on a polarimeter. Because one is assigned and the other observed, all four combinations exist: R[+], R[-], S[+] and S[-].'},
  {t:'Yes — R is always dextrorotatory', correct:false,
   why:'This is the standard confusion in this topic, and it is encouraged by both labels using a notion of direction. The two directions refer to different things: one is the rotation of substituents in space, the other the rotation of a plane of light.'},
  {t:'Yes — R is always levorotatory', correct:false,
   why:'False for the same reason, with the added problem that it contradicts the previous option, which shows that neither fixed pairing can hold.'},
  {t:'Only for molecules with a single chiral centre', correct:false,
   why:'The independence holds regardless of how many centres a molecule has. Nothing about adding centres creates a link between an assigned configuration and a measured rotation.'}],
 teach:'Two naming systems describe chirality and they answer different questions. The R and S convention describes absolute configuration, the actual three-dimensional arrangement, and it is worked out on paper from atomic numbers. The d and l notation, written as plus and minus, describes optical activity, which is a physical property measured with a polarimeter and reported as a rotation to the right or the left. A full designation carries both, as in R(-)-epinephrine, which has R configuration and rotates light to the left. An older D and L system based on glyceraldehyde also exists and is separate from both.',
 teachImg:'st_config_rotation',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 17–18',
 quote:'A compound can be R[+], R[-], S[+], or S[-]'},

{id:'st-5', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'terms', concept:'enantiomer-vs-diastereomer',
 tags:['definition','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Two stereoisomers have two chiral centres. They differ at one centre and match at the other. What is their relationship?',
 options:[
  {t:'Diastereomers', correct:true,
   why:'A mirror image inverts every centre, so matching at one of them rules out an enantiomeric relationship. What remains is a non-superimposable, non-mirror-image pair, which is the definition of diastereomers.'},
  {t:'Enantiomers', correct:false,
   why:'Enantiomers are complete mirror images, so all chiral centres would have to be inverted. Inverting only some of them is exactly what produces diastereomers instead.'},
  {t:'A racemic mixture', correct:false,
   why:'A racemate is a 1:1 mixture of two enantiomers, so the term describes the composition of a sample rather than the relationship between two structures.'},
  {t:'Identical compounds', correct:false,
   why:'They differ at one centre, so they are distinct compounds, and unlike enantiomers they differ in ordinary physical properties as well, since diastereomers are not mirror images.'}],
 teach:'Counting inverted centres settles the relationship. Invert all of them and the result is the mirror image, so the pair are enantiomers, and they share boiling point, melting point, density and refractive index. Invert some but not all and the pair are diastereomers, which are not mirror images and therefore differ in ordinary physical properties as well as in biology. Two chiral centres give four stereoisomers arranged as two enantiomeric pairs, with every cross-pair being diastereomeric. Diastereomers also arise without chiral centres at all, from double bonds and from ring systems.',
 teachImg:'st_diastereomers',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 14, 21',
 quote:'Enantiomers are nonsuperimposable mirror images · Diastereomers: Nonsuperimposable, non-mirror image isomers.'},

{id:'st-6', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'terms', concept:'enantiomer-properties',
 tags:['reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Enantiomers share nearly identical boiling point, melting point, density and refractive index. Where do they differ?',
 options:[
  {t:'In the direction they rotate plane-polarized light, and in how they interact with chiral biological molecules', correct:true,
   why:'Telling enantiomers apart requires a chiral environment. Plane-polarized light provides one, which is why polarimetry works, and receptors and enzymes provide another, which is why one enantiomer can carry the activity and the other little or none.'},
  {t:'In molecular formula and molecular weight', correct:false,
   why:'Stereoisomers contain the same number and kinds of atoms by definition, differing only in three-dimensional arrangement, so formula and weight are necessarily identical.'},
  {t:'In the number of chiral centres they contain', correct:false,
   why:'Enantiomers have the same centres in the same places; what differs is the configuration at each one. A difference in the number of centres would make them different compounds altogether.'},
  {t:'In their ultraviolet absorption maxima', correct:false,
   why:'Ultraviolet absorption is an achiral measurement and cannot separate them, in the same way that boiling point cannot. Optical rotation is the exception because polarized light is itself chiral.'}],
 teach:'Enantiomers are distinguishable only by something that is itself chiral, which explains both the analytical method and the pharmacology at once. A polarimeter works because plane-polarized light is chiral; ordinary physical measurements do not. Receptors and enzymes are built from chiral amino acids, so they also discriminate, and one enantiomer is usually many times more active than the other. That enantiomer is the eutomer, with R(-)-epinephrine given as the example. The same discrimination extends to absorption, metabolism and receptor binding, so two enantiomers can behave as different drugs.',
 teachImg:'st_optical',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 16–19',
 quote:'Enantiomers have nearly identical physical properties: Same BP, MP, density, refractive index'},

{id:'st-7', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'terms', concept:'easson-stedman',
 tags:['definition','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What does the Easson–Stedman hypothesis propose?',
 options:[
  {t:'The more potent enantiomer is the one able to make a three-point interaction with the receptor', correct:true,
   why:'Two points of contact can be satisfied by either enantiomer, because a two-point fit does not distinguish handedness. A minimum of three simultaneous interactions can be achieved by only one of them, which is the geometric reason activity concentrates in a single isomer.'},
  {t:'Both enantiomers bind equally but only one is absorbed', correct:false,
   why:'The hypothesis is about binding geometry at the receptor surface. Enantiomers can differ in absorption as well, which the deck notes separately, but that is not what this model explains.'},
  {t:'Racemic mixtures are always more potent than single enantiomers', correct:false,
   why:'A racemate is half eutomer, so it dilutes the active isomer with one that is less active. Levalbuterol needs a quarter of the albuterol dose for the same bronchodilation, which shows the effect of removing the less active isomer.'},
  {t:'Potency is determined by optical rotation direction', correct:false,
   why:'Optical rotation is a measured physical property with no fixed relationship to either configuration or potency. The eutomer can be the dextrorotatory or the levorotatory isomer.'}],
 teach:'This is the structural reason stereochemistry matters clinically rather than only descriptively. A receptor surface offering three binding points can be matched fully by one enantiomer and only partially by its mirror image, so the two are not interchangeable even though they contain identical atoms. The consequences run through the deck: ethambutol is more potent as the S,S isomer than the R,R; the E isomer of triprolidine is a thousand-fold more potent than the Z; levalbuterol achieves with a quarter of the dose what racemic albuterol needs; and esomeprazole is the single S isomer of omeprazole.',
 teachImg:'st_biological',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 3, 19–20; triprolidine slide 25',
 quote:'Easson-Stedman Hypothesis: The more potent enantiomer must be involved in a minimum of three intermolecular interactions with the receptor surface. Whereas the less potent enantiomer only interacts with two sites'},

{id:'st-8', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'stereo', sub:'terms', concept:'ez-isomerism',
 tags:['definition','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'When is E/Z notation used instead of cis/trans?',
 options:[
  {t:'When there are more than two different substituents on the double bond, so priority rules are needed', correct:true,
   why:'Cis and trans work when it is obvious which groups are being compared. With several different substituents that is ambiguous, so each carbon of the double bond has its two groups ranked by atomic number, and Z means the higher-priority pair are together while E means they are opposite.'},
  {t:'Only for ring systems', correct:false,
   why:'Ring systems show cis and trans isomerism of their own, with 1,2-dichlorocyclohexane as the example, but E and Z notation was introduced for double bonds carrying several different substituents.'},
  {t:'Only when the molecule has a chiral centre', correct:false,
   why:'E and Z describe geometry around a double bond and need no chiral centre at all. A molecule can show E/Z isomerism while being achiral, which is why both fall under diastereomers rather than enantiomers.'},
  {t:'Whenever the molecule is optically active', correct:false,
   why:'Optical activity arises from chirality, a separate phenomenon. Double bond geometry is fixed by restricted rotation rather than by handedness.'}],
 teach:'Both notations answer the same question, which groups sit on the same side of the double bond, and they differ in how the groups being compared are chosen. Cis and trans compare the obvious pair; E and Z apply the same atomic-number priority rules used for R and S to each carbon of the double bond, then ask whether the two winners are together, zusammen, or opposite, entgegen. Restricted rotation is what makes the arrangement permanent and therefore a real isomerism, and the consequences can be large: the E isomer of triprolidine is a thousand-fold more potent an antihistamine than the Z.',
 teachImg:'st_ez',
 cite:'Stereochemistry (081926_Stereochemistry.pdf) slides 22–25; ring systems slide 26',
 quote:'Z (zusammen) means together · E (entgegen) means opposite'},

{id:'dd-1', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'sar', concept:'sar-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What does a structure–activity relationship study establish?',
 options:[
  {t:'How changes in chemical structure change biological activity', correct:true,
   why:'The method is systematic modification: alter a functional group, measure what happens to activity, and repeat. What it establishes is which parts of a molecule the activity depends on, which is what makes further modification purposeful rather than random.'},
  {t:'How a drug is formulated into a dosage form', correct:false,
   why:'Formulation concerns the tablet, capsule or suspension a drug is delivered in. It affects dissolution and therefore absorption, but it changes nothing about the molecule itself.'},
  {t:'How quickly a drug is eliminated from the body', correct:false,
   why:'That is a pharmacokinetic measurement. Structure does influence it, since physicochemical properties follow from functional groups, but a structure-activity study asks about biological activity rather than clearance.'},
  {t:'The market value of a drug candidate', correct:false,
   why:'This is a laboratory method rather than a commercial assessment. The relevant stages in the discovery process are preclinical and clinical testing, which come after the structural work.'}],
 teach:'Brown and Fraser established the approach in 1869 by converting tertiary amines into quaternary ammonium compounds and observing muscle relaxation, and the conclusion drawn is carefully limited: it is not true that all quaternary ammonium compounds are muscle relaxants, but it is true that molecular structure influences biological activity. That distinction matters, since a single modification shows a correlation rather than a rule. The same work produced a second observation still used in design, that antagonists such as tubocurarine are generally larger than the native neurotransmitter they block, in this case acetylcholine.',
 cite:'Introduction to SAR (082426_IntroductiontoSAR.pdf) slides 3–4, 7–8',
 quote:'Systematic modifications of molecules by altering the functional groups and determining how these modifications affect the biological activity'},

{id:'dd-2', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'bio', concept:'bioisostere-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'slide',
 stem:'How does Burger define bioisosteres?',
 options:[
  {t:'Compounds or groups with near equal molecular shape and volume and approximately the same electronic distribution, which show similar biological properties', correct:true,
   why:'The definition has three parts: similar shape and volume, similar distribution of electrons, and consequently similar physical properties such as hydrophobicity, which produce related biological properties. Shape and electronics together are what allow one group to stand in for another at a binding site.'},
  {t:'Compounds with identical molecular formulas but different connectivity', correct:false,
   why:'That describes structural isomers, which share a formula and differ in how the atoms are joined. Bioisosteres need not share a formula at all, and non-classical ones differ even in atom count.'},
  {t:'Any two drugs used for the same indication', correct:false,
   why:'Shared indication says nothing about structure. Two antihypertensives may work through entirely different targets, while bioisosterism is a relationship between groups within molecules.'},
  {t:'Mirror-image forms of the same molecule', correct:false,
   why:'Those are enantiomers, which have identical connectivity and differ only in three-dimensional arrangement. Bioisosteres are different groups that behave similarly, not the same group reflected.'}],
 teach:'Bioisosteric replacement is a design tool applied to a problem rather than a classification exercise, and three uses are named: improving pharmacokinetic properties by altering physicochemical ones, improving the pharmacological action, and removing unwanted side effects. Each depends on the substitute group preserving enough shape and electronic character that the target still recognises the molecule, while changing the properties that were causing trouble. Ranitidine to nizatidine is the clearest instance, where swapping furan for thiazole changes bioavailability without changing what the drug does.',
 teachImg:'bi_burger_def',
 cite:'Bioisosterism (082626_Bioisosterism.pdf) slides 3–4',
 quote:'Compounds/groups that possess near equal molecular shapes and volumes, approximately the same distribution of electrons'},

{id:'dd-3', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'bio', concept:'classical-vs-nonclassical',
 tags:['definition','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What separates classical from non-classical bioisosteres?',
 options:[
  {t:'Classical bioisosteres share valence electron configuration and atom count; non-classical ones do not, yet still mimic the original group', correct:true,
   why:'Classical groups are organised by valency, running from monovalent atoms and groups through divalent, trivalent and tetrasubstituted to ring equivalents. Non-classical ones are defined by the absence of that correspondence while still producing similar biological activity.'},
  {t:'Classical bioisosteres are naturally occurring and non-classical are synthetic', correct:false,
   why:'Origin plays no part in the distinction, which is structural. Both kinds appear in synthetic drugs throughout the deck.'},
  {t:'Classical bioisosteres improve potency and non-classical improve solubility', correct:false,
   why:'Either kind can be used for either purpose. The categories describe how closely the substitute resembles the original group electronically, not what the substitution is trying to achieve.'},
  {t:'Classical bioisosteres apply only to rings', correct:false,
   why:'Ring equivalents are one of five classical subcategories, alongside monovalent, divalent, trivalent and tetrasubstituted groups. Sulfadiazine and sulfamethoxazole are the ring-equivalent example.'}],
 teach:'The two categories differ in how much the substitute has to resemble the original. Classical replacement keeps valence electron configuration and atom count, so fluorine can replace hydrogen because both are monovalent, and one aromatic ring can replace another under Hinsberg\'s ring-equivalent idea. Non-classical replacement abandons that correspondence and relies on the groups behaving alike, which is why it divides into exchangeable groups and rings versus non-cyclic structures. Tetrazole for carboxylic acid is the standard non-classical case, and sumatriptan against naratriptan shows the ring versus non-cyclic form.',
 teachImg:'bi_classes',
 cite:'Bioisosterism (082626_Bioisosterism.pdf) slides 5, 12',
 quote:'Nonclassical Bioisosteres: Atoms or functional groups that do not have similar valence electron configurations and number of atoms (but may display similar biological activities)'},

{id:'dd-4', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'bio', concept:'tetrazole-carboxylic-acid',
 tags:['recall','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Tetrazole is used as a bioisosteric replacement for which group, and in which drug class?',
 options:[
  {t:'Carboxylic acid, in the angiotensin II receptor blockers', correct:true,
   why:'Both groups are acidic, so both lose a proton at physiological pH and end up as anions carrying a delocalised negative charge \u2014 delocalised meaning the charge is spread across several atoms instead of sitting on one. The carboxylate spreads it over two oxygens and the tetrazolate over four nitrogens, and in both cases the result is a flat, similarly sized anion of comparable acidity. A binding site that recognises a carboxylate is reading charge and shape rather than counting atoms, so it accepts the tetrazole. Losartan is the named example and the drug is orally active.'},
  {t:'A primary amine, in the beta blockers', correct:false,
   why:'This one reverses the acid-base direction, so it is a chemistry error rather than a recall error. A tetrazole donates its ring proton and becomes an anion; a primary amine accepts a proton and becomes a cation. At physiological pH the two carry opposite charges, so a site that binds a tetrazolate by electrostatic attraction would repel an amine and the other way round. Beta blockers do carry an amine, which is what makes the option look plausible, but a bioisostere has to reproduce the ionisation behaviour of the group it replaces, not merely occupy the same position.'},
  {t:'A phenol, in the catecholamines', correct:false,
   why:'The principle is right and the pairing is crossed, which makes this the closest wrong answer. Isoproterenol\'s meta hydroxyl is genuinely replaced in this material \u2014 but by a sulfonamide, chosen because the two have almost equal pKa values and similar hydrogen bonding potential. So a phenol is a real exchangeable group here; its partner is the sulfonamide, one slide earlier, and the tetrazole belongs to the carboxylic acid instead.'},
  {t:'An ester, in the ACE inhibitors', correct:false,
   why:'This is a recall slip rather than a reasoning one: the ACE inhibitors do appear as a non-classical example, so the association is real, but the groups exchanged there are the sulfhydryl and the carboxylic acid, captopril carrying the thiol against enalapril and lisinopril\'s dicarboxylates. No ester is involved. If the drug class felt familiar but the groups did not, that points at learning the examples by drug name rather than by the pair of groups being swapped.'}],
 teach:[
  {h:'The mechanism',
   t:'A carboxylate and a deprotonated tetrazole are built from different atoms yet present the same thing to a receptor. A carboxylic acid gives up its proton to leave COO-, whose negative charge is delocalised over the two oxygens: delocalised means spread across several atoms rather than held on one, so neither oxygen carries a full unit of charge and the group behaves as a single flat anion. A tetrazole gives up its ring proton to leave a tetrazolate, whose charge is delocalised the same way over four nitrogens. Both are acidic at physiological pH, both finish as flat anions of similar size, and a binding site that recognises a carboxylate by its charge distribution and shape responds to the tetrazolate as well.'},
  {h:'Classical against non-classical',
   t:'Classical bioisosterism matches atoms. Fluorine replaces hydrogen because its valency is 1, so atom counts and valence electron configurations line up between the two, and the classical subcategories are sorted on exactly that basis: monovalent, divalent, trivalent, tetrasubstituted, and ring equivalents. Non-classical bioisosterism drops the atom-matching requirement and keeps only the outcome, covering groups that do not have similar valence electron configurations or numbers of atoms but may still display similar biological activity.'},
  {h:'Why this example is non-classical',
   t:'Carboxylate and tetrazole differ on both of the counts that definition names. Their atom counts differ: a carboxylate is a carbon with two oxygens, three atoms carrying the acidic function, while a tetrazolate is a ring of five, four nitrogens and a carbon. Their valence electron configurations differ as well, since oxygen carries six outer-shell electrons and forms two bonds while nitrogen carries five and forms three. So neither requirement for a classical pairing is met, and what survives the swap is only the behaviour: same acidity, same delocalised anion, same response from the binding site. Within non-classical this is the exchangeable-groups subcategory, alongside isoproterenol\u2019s hydroxyl-for-sulfonamide swap and the ACE inhibitors\u2019 sulfhydryl-for-carboxylic-acid swap. The other subcategory is rings against non-cyclic structures, where the triptans sit.'},
  {h:'What the swap buys',
   t:'Losartan still blocks the angiotensin II receptor while carrying no carboxylic acid at all, so the recognition survives the substitution. Across the series the potency runs from S-8303 at an IC50 of 15 micromolar to losartan at 0.019 micromolar, with the carboxyl-for-tetrazole exchange as the last step shown, and losartan is orally active.'}],
 teachImg:'bi_tetrazole',
 cite:'Bioisosterism (082626_Bioisosterism.pdf) slide 14',
 quote:'Tetrazole: A bioisosteric replacement of carboxylic acid. · Angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) are used as antihypertensive agents'},

{id:'dd-5', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'bio', concept:'ranitidine-nizatidine',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Replacing the furan ring of ranitidine with a thiazole gives nizatidine. What did that achieve?',
 options:[
  {t:'Oral bioavailability rose from roughly 50% to roughly 90%', correct:true,
   why:'Furan and thiazole are ring equivalents, so the molecule is still recognised at the target, but the pharmacokinetic properties differ. The deck notes a second consequence as well: nizatidine has a short half-life, so the substitution changed more than one property at once.'},
  {t:'Potency at the receptor increased tenfold', correct:false,
   why:'Bioisosteric replacement preserves recognition at the target rather than improving it, which is what allows the substitution in the first place. What changed here was how much drug reaches the circulation.'},
  {t:'The drug became a prodrug requiring bioactivation', correct:false,
   why:'Nizatidine is active as given. Prodrug design is a different strategy for the same problem of poor bioavailability, used for enalapril and valacyclovir.'},
  {t:'It removed the need for hepatic metabolism entirely', correct:false,
   why:'Nothing in the substitution eliminates metabolism. The short half-life noted for nizatidine points the other way, toward continued clearance.'}],
 teach:'This is the clearest demonstration of why bioisosterism is a pharmacokinetic tool. Swapping one heteroaromatic ring for another is a classical ring-equivalent substitution, chosen because it preserves shape and electronic distribution so the drug keeps working. What changes is everything the structure governs afterwards, and more than one property moves at once: bioavailability roughly doubles while the half-life shortens. That is the general caution with this technique, since a substitution made to fix one property will move others, and the whole profile has to be reassessed rather than the single target property.',
 teachImg:'bi_ranitidine',
 cite:'Bioisosterism (082626_Bioisosterism.pdf) slides 7, 11',
 quote:'Ranitidine Furan · Oral bioavailability: ~50% · Nizatidine (Axid) Thiazole · Oral bioavailability: ~90%. · Short half-life'},

{id:'dd-6', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'bio', concept:'fluorouracil-bioisostere',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'5-Fluorouracil replaces a hydrogen with fluorine. Why does that substitution work as a bioisostere, and what is the result?',
 options:[
  {t:'Fluorine is monovalent and close in size to hydrogen, so the molecule is still recognized, but it inhibits thymidylate synthetase', correct:true,
   why:'Fluorine needs one more electron to close its shell, so like hydrogen it forms a single bond, which makes this a classical monovalent substitution. The enzyme accepts the molecule as though it were uracil and is then blocked, which is why 5-fluorouracil acts as an antimetabolite.'},
  {t:'Fluorine is divalent and mimics oxygen', correct:false,
   why:'Fluorine forms one bond rather than two. Divalent substitution is a separate classical subcategory, and confusing the two would predict the wrong position in the molecule.'},
  {t:'Fluorine increases water solubility so the drug is excreted faster', correct:false,
   why:'Faster excretion would work against a cytotoxic agent rather than for it, and the mechanism given is enzyme inhibition. Fluorine is also not a strongly solubilising substituent.'},
  {t:'Fluorine makes the molecule chiral and improves selectivity', correct:false,
   why:'Chirality requires four different groups on a tetrahedral carbon, and this substitution is on a planar aromatic ring. The selectivity comes from the enzyme being fooled by the resemblance to uracil.'}],
 teach:'Bioisosterism is used here to sabotage rather than to improve, which shows the technique is neutral about intent. The requirement is the same either way, that the substitute preserve enough shape and electronic character for the target to accept the molecule. Thymidylate synthase converts dUMP to dTMP, a step required for DNA replication, so blocking it stops cells from dividing and gives a treatment for colorectal and pancreatic cancer. This also links back to the metabolism material, since dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase normally inactivates more than 80 percent of a 5-fluorouracil dose and is polymorphic.',
 teachImg:'bi_fluorouracil',
 cite:'Bioisosterism (082626_Bioisosterism.pdf) slides 6, 9',
 quote:'5-Fluorouracil (Adrucil) inhibits thymidylate synthetase · Function: Responsible for conversion of dUMP to dTMP'},

{id:'dd-7', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'disc', sub:'sources', concept:'drug-sources',
 tags:['recall'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which of these is a recognized source of drugs in the drug discovery material?',
 options:[
  {t:'Natural products from plants and microorganisms, and molecules based on mammalian biochemical pathways', correct:true,
   why:'These are the natural product categories named, alongside synthetic products as a separate source. Digoxin from foxglove, quinidine and quinine from cinchona bark and morphine from opium poppy are the plant examples; penicillin the microbial one; and epinephrine, insulin and levothyroxine those based on mammalian pathways.'},
  {t:'Only total chemical synthesis from petrochemicals', correct:false,
   why:'Synthetic products are one recognised source but not the only one, and the word only is what makes this wrong. Several major drugs in the deck came from natural material first and were synthesised afterwards.'},
  {t:'Only computational design with no laboratory testing', correct:false,
   why:'Rational design with computers is a genuine approach, used to discover zanamivir and oseltamivir as neuraminidase inhibitors, but it is one route among several and does not replace preclinical and clinical testing.'},
  {t:'Only reformulation of existing marketed products', correct:false,
   why:'Reformulation changes the dosage form rather than discovering a molecule. Modifying an existing drug is closer to the bioisosterism and prodrug work covered separately.'}],
 teach:'Sources and approaches are two different lists, and questions here often mix them. The sources are where a molecule came from: plants, microorganisms, mammalian biochemical pathways, and synthetic products. The approaches are how it was found: without a lead molecule, as penicillin was by accident and chlordiazepoxide from a series of quinazoline N-oxides; from natural products, as aspirin was from willow bark; by random screening, as prontosil was; by rational design, as the neuraminidase inhibitors were; from drug metabolism studies, which gave sulfanilamide and fexofenadine; and from observation of side effects, which gave the phenothiazine antipsychotics and sildenafil.',
 teachImg:'dd_drug_sources',
 cite:'Drug Discovery (082026_Drugdiscovery.pdf) slides 9, 17',
 quote:'Natural products: Plants · Micro organisms · Based on mammalian biochemical pathways · Other sources: Synthetic products',
 note:'The option text was corrected here: slide 9 lists plants, microorganisms and mammalian biochemical pathways, and does not name marine organisms.'},

{id:'dr-1', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'recept', sub:'bonds', concept:'bond-strength-order',
 tags:['ranking','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Rank these drug–receptor interactions from strongest to weakest.',
 options:[
  {t:'Covalent (50–150 kcal/mol), ionic (5–10), hydrogen bond (2–5), hydrophobic (0.5–1)', correct:true,
   why:'The four ranges are given directly and they do not overlap, so the order is unambiguous. The span matters as much as the sequence: covalent is roughly a hundred times a hydrophobic contact, which is why one is effectively permanent and the other momentary.'},
  {t:'Ionic, covalent, hydrophobic, hydrogen bond', correct:false,
   why:'Two errors. Covalent is strongest by a wide margin rather than second, and hydrogen bonding at 2 to 5 is stronger than hydrophobic interaction at 0.5 to 1.'},
  {t:'Hydrogen bond, ionic, covalent, hydrophobic', correct:false,
   why:'This puts the strongest interaction in third place. Hydrogen bonding is third in the real order, not first, though multiple hydrogen bonds commonly form between a drug and a receptor and their energies add up.'},
  {t:'Hydrophobic, hydrogen bond, ionic, covalent', correct:false,
   why:'This is the correct sequence written backwards, weakest to strongest. It is worth recognising as the reversed form rather than as a different ordering.'}],
 teach:'The four interaction types divide into one that is effectively permanent and three that are not. A covalent bond at 50 to 150 kcal/mol does not dissociate, so recovery depends on the receptor being destroyed by endocytosis and chemical destruction rather than on the drug leaving. The other three are reversible, which is what allows a drug to dissociate as plasma concentration falls. Strength alone does not decide importance, since the weak interactions occur in numbers: multiple hydrogen bonds add up, and a hydrophobic pocket makes many contacts at once. Atorvastatin binding HMG-CoA reductase uses ionic, ion-dipole, hydrogen bonding and hydrophobic contacts together.',
 teachImg:'dr_ligand_residues',
 cite:'Drug–Receptor Interactions (082426_DrugReceptorInteractions.pdf) slides 4–8',
 quote:'Covalent Bond · Strongest of bonds · Strength: 50-150 kcal/mol'},

{id:'dr-2', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'recept', sub:'bonds', concept:'covalent-irreversible',
 tags:['reasoning','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Phenoxybenzamine blocks alpha-adrenoceptors covalently. What follows clinically?',
 options:[
  {t:'The blockade is irreversible, so effect persists until new receptor is synthesized', correct:true,
   why:'A covalent bond does not dissociate, so the receptor is not freed by the drug washing out. Recovery depends on the receptor being destroyed and replaced, which means the duration of effect outlasts the plasma concentration entirely.'},
  {t:'The blockade reverses as soon as plasma concentration falls', correct:false,
   why:'That is how the reversible interactions behave, ionic, hydrogen and hydrophobic, where binding is an equilibrium and falling free drug shifts it toward dissociation. A covalent bond is not part of that equilibrium.'},
  {t:'The drug acts as an agonist rather than a blocker', correct:false,
   why:'The type of bond decides how long the drug stays, not what it does while bound. Phenoxybenzamine blocks the receptor, and binding covalently makes that blockade permanent rather than turning it into activation.'},
  {t:'Effect is weaker than with hydrogen bonding', correct:false,
   why:'The comparison runs the other way by an order of magnitude or more: 50 to 150 kcal/mol for a covalent bond against 2 to 5 for a hydrogen bond.'}],
 teach:'Phenoxybenzamine works through an aziridinium ion, an electron-deficient species that is attacked by a nucleophile on the receptor. That nucleophile is part of an amino acid side chain, a cysteine thiol, a serine hydroxyl or a lysine amino group, which links this reaction to the electrophile and nucleophile chemistry running through the metabolism and toxicity lectures. The clinical signature of covalent binding is a mismatch between the drug\'s half-life and its duration of action, since the effect is governed by how fast the tissue replaces the receptor rather than by how fast the body clears the drug.',
 teachImg:'dr_covalent_ex',
 cite:'Drug–Receptor Interactions (082426_DrugReceptorInteractions.pdf) slides 4–5',
 quote:'Irreversible binding: Receptor\'s are destroyed by endocytosis and chemical destruction'},

{id:'dr-3', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'recept', sub:'aa', concept:'aa-ionic-interaction',
 tags:['reasoning','structure','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A drug carries a carboxylate group. Which amino acid side chain can form an ionic interaction with it?',
 options:[
  {t:'Lysine, through its protonated amino group', correct:true,
   why:'An ionic interaction needs two opposite charges. A carboxylate is negative, and the lysine side chain amine is protonated and positive at physiological pH, so the two attract. Arginine serves the same role through its guanidine group.'},
  {t:'Leucine, through its isobutyl side chain', correct:false,
   why:'Leucine is nonpolar and carries no ionizable group, so it can only take part in hydrophobic or van der Waals contacts. Those are real interactions at 0.5 to 1 kcal/mol, but not ionic ones.'},
  {t:'Glutamate, through its carboxylate', correct:false,
   why:'Both groups would carry a negative charge, so they repel. Glutamate and aspartate are the residues a positively charged drug would pair with, which is the same rule applied in the opposite direction.'},
  {t:'Phenylalanine, through its benzene ring', correct:false,
   why:'An uncharged aromatic ring contributes hydrophobic interaction, and aromatic rings are named as hydrophobic contributors in the atorvastatin example. Without a charge there is nothing for an ionic interaction to form between.'}],
 teach:'Ionic interactions are read off the charges, so the method is to identify the ionizable groups on both sides and check the signs at physiological pH. A negatively charged drug pairs with lysine or arginine; a positively charged drug pairs with aspartate or glutamate. At 5 to 10 kcal/mol these are the strongest of the reversible interactions, so they usually anchor the binding while hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic contacts refine the fit. Captopril binding ACE shows all three at once: ionic interactions, hydrogen bonding and a hydrophobic pocket, with its thiol ionized to the thiolate.',
 teachImg:'dr_ionic',
 cite:'Drug–Receptor Interactions (082426_DrugReceptorInteractions.pdf) slides 6, 12',
 quote:'Interaction of a negatively charged drug with positively charged aminoacids (lysine, arginine)'},

{id:'dr-4', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'recept', sub:'aa', concept:'aa-hbond-donors-acceptors',
 tags:['reasoning','structure'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which amino acid side chains can participate in hydrogen bonding with a drug?',
 options:[
  {t:'Serine, threonine, tyrosine, asparagine and glutamine', correct:true,
   why:'Each carries a hydroxyl or an amide, so each has a hydrogen bound to an electronegative atom to donate and a lone pair to accept. Those are the two requirements, and a side chain meeting either one can take part.'},
  {t:'Alanine, valine, leucine and isoleucine', correct:false,
   why:'These are the nonpolar hydrocarbon side chains, with no electronegative atom and therefore no polar hydrogen to donate. They contribute the hydrophobic interactions that line a binding pocket instead.'},
  {t:'Phenylalanine and tryptophan only', correct:false,
   why:'Tryptophan does have an indole nitrogen-hydrogen that can donate, so the pairing is half right. Phenylalanine is a plain aromatic ring with no heteroatom, and restricting hydrogen bonding to these two leaves out the hydroxyl and amide side chains that do most of it.'},
  {t:'Glycine and proline', correct:false,
   why:'Glycine has only a hydrogen as its side chain, so there is nothing to bond with. Proline\'s nitrogen is locked into its ring and the peptide chain, which is also why proline disrupts secondary structure.'}],
 teach:'A hydrogen bond needs a donor, meaning a hydrogen attached to an electronegative atom such as oxygen or nitrogen, and an acceptor, meaning a lone pair on such an atom. Reading a side chain for those two features identifies its capability without recall: hydroxyls on serine, threonine and tyrosine can do both, and the amides of asparagine and glutamine likewise. At 2 to 5 kcal/mol a single hydrogen bond is modest, but multiple hydrogen bonds usually form between a drug and a receptor and their contributions add up, which is why atorvastatin makes several alongside its ionic and ion-dipole contacts.',
 teachImg:'dr_hbond',
 cite:'Drug–Receptor Interactions (082426_DrugReceptorInteractions.pdf) slides 7, 11',
 quote:'Usually multiple hydrogen bonds are formed between drugs and receptors so they can add up'},

{id:'dr-5', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'recept', sub:'aa', concept:'hydrophobic-pocket',
 tags:['reasoning','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Captopril binding to ACE includes a hydrophobic pocket contact. Which side chains line such a pocket?',
 options:[
  {t:'Nonpolar side chains such as valine, leucine, isoleucine and phenylalanine', correct:true,
   why:'A hydrophobic pocket excludes water and pairs nonpolar regions of the drug with nonpolar side chains. The interaction is van der Waals or London forces, arising from a temporary imbalance of charge distribution that induces a dipole in the neighbouring molecule.'},
  {t:'Charged side chains such as aspartate and lysine', correct:false,
   why:'Charged residues are usually solvent exposed rather than buried in a water-excluding pocket, and they form the ionic interactions in the same binding site. Captopril makes both kinds, but at different points of contact.'},
  {t:'Polar side chains such as serine and asparagine', correct:false,
   why:'These favour hydrogen bonding, the third interaction captopril makes with ACE. Polar side chains in a hydrophobic pocket would be energetically unfavourable.'},
  {t:'Cysteine, through disulfide formation', correct:false,
   why:'A disulfide is a covalent bond at 50 to 150 kcal/mol, a hundred times a hydrophobic contact. Captopril does carry a thiol, but it binds the active-site zinc rather than forming a disulfide.'}],
 teach:'Hydrophobic interactions are the weakest at 0.5 to 1 kcal/mol, and they matter through number rather than individual strength. Two nonpolar surfaces in contact exclude water between them and are held by induced dipoles, so a pocket making many such contacts can contribute substantially to binding. Atorvastatin is the clearest case: its three phenyl rings and isopropyl side chain all interact hydrophobically with the enzyme. Captopril shows the general pattern, where a single drug uses ionic interactions to anchor, hydrogen bonds to orient, and a hydrophobic pocket to add affinity across a large surface.',
 teachImg:'dr_captopril_ace',
 cite:'Drug–Receptor Interactions (082426_DrugReceptorInteractions.pdf) slides 8, 11–12',
 quote:'Hydrophobic Interactions · Van der Waals forces or London forces · Strength: 0.5-1 kcal/mol'},

{id:'pc-1', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'acidbase', concept:'pka-direction-acids',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'For acidic functional groups, how does pKa relate to acid strength?',
 options:[
  {t:'Lower pKa means a stronger acid', correct:true,
   why:'pKa is the negative logarithm of the dissociation constant, so a smaller pKa corresponds to a larger Ka and more complete dissociation. The negative logarithm is what inverts the direction and makes this feel backwards.'},
  {t:'Higher pKa means a stronger acid', correct:false,
   why:'That is the rule for bases, where higher pKa does mean greater strength. Applying the base rule to an acid is the standard error here, which is why both directions are set out together on slide 18.'},
  {t:'pKa is unrelated to acid strength', correct:false,
   why:'pKa is derived directly from the dissociation constant, so it measures nothing else. What it does not tell you on its own is how ionized the drug is, since that also depends on the pH of the environment.'},
  {t:'pKa only applies to bases', correct:false,
   why:'Both acids and bases are described by pKa, which is what allows them to be compared on one scale. The conjugate acid of a base is what the value refers to in that case.'}],
 teach:'One pair of statements covers both directions: lower pKa means a stronger acid, higher pKa means a stronger base. Holding them together prevents applying one to the other, which is the usual error. Strength and ionization are separate questions, though, and pKa alone answers only the first. How much of a drug is ionized depends on pKa and on the pH of the surroundings, which is what the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation relates. Substituents shift pKa predictably, since electron-withdrawing groups increase acidity by stabilising the conjugate base and electron-donating groups decrease it.',
 teachImg:'pp_pka_trend',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 1 (082726_PhysicochemicalProp1.pdf) slides 9, 18',
 quote:'Therefore, lower the pKa for acids, higher is their strength'},

{id:'pc-2', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'acidbase', concept:'aryl-vs-alkyl-amine-basicity',
 tags:['reasoning','mechanism'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Why are aryl amines less basic than aliphatic amines?',
 options:[
  {t:'The nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring, so it is less available to accept a proton', correct:true,
   why:'Basicity requires a lone pair free to bind a proton. When the nitrogen sits on an aromatic ring its lone pair interacts with the ring electron cloud and is spread over the system, so less of it is available at the nitrogen.'},
  {t:'The aromatic ring physically blocks the nitrogen', correct:false,
   why:'The effect is electronic rather than steric. Steric hindrance does influence reactivity in other settings, but here the lone pair itself is less available regardless of how accessible the nitrogen is.'},
  {t:'Aryl amines carry a permanent positive charge', correct:false,
   why:'A permanent positive charge describes a quaternary ammonium compound, which cannot accept a proton at all because the nitrogen already has four bonds. An aryl amine is neutral and weakly basic.'},
  {t:'Aryl amines have a higher pKa than aliphatic amines', correct:false,
   why:'The relationship is inverted. Higher pKa means a stronger base, so a less basic aryl amine has the lower pKa of the two.'}],
 teach:'Availability of the lone pair decides basicity, so anything that delocalises it weakens the base. An aliphatic amine keeps its lone pair localised on nitrogen and is a reasonably strong base; an aryl amine shares it with the ring and is much weaker. The same reasoning identifies other groups: an amide nitrogen is essentially non-basic because its lone pair is delocalised into the adjacent carbonyl, which is why amides appear among the neutral functional groups, while a biguanide is strongly basic. What the nitrogen is bonded to, and whether its lone pair is pulled into a ring or into an adjacent carbonyl, settles the basicity of a nitrogen that is not on any memorised list.',
 teachImg:'pp_basic_groups',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 1 (082726_PhysicochemicalProp1.pdf) slides 15–16',
 quote:'The lone pair of electrons on nitrogen in aryl amines interacts with the electron cloud of aryl amines'},

{id:'pc-3', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'acidbase', concept:'neutral-groups',
 tags:['structure','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which group is neutral at physiological pH, contributing neither acidity nor basicity?',
 options:[
  {t:'An ester', correct:true,
   why:'An ester has no acidic hydrogen to donate and no available lone pair to accept one, so it neither ionizes nor accepts a proton at physiological pH. Ketones, aldehydes, amides, ureas and hydrocarbons are neutral for the same reason.'},
  {t:'A carboxylic acid', correct:false,
   why:'A carboxylic acid is the archetypal acidic group, ionizing to the carboxylate. At physiological pH it is almost entirely in that ionized form, since its pKa is well below 7.4.'},
  {t:'An aliphatic amine', correct:false,
   why:'An aliphatic amine is basic, accepting a proton to give the ammonium conjugate acid. Its lone pair is localised on nitrogen and available, which is what makes it the stronger kind of amine base.'},
  {t:'A phenol', correct:false,
   why:'A phenol is weakly acidic, with a pKa around 9 to 11, ionizing to the phenolate anion. Its acidity comes from the aromatic ring delocalising the negative charge of the conjugate base. Plain alcohols, by contrast, are treated as neutral.'}],
 teach:'Sorting a molecule into acidic, basic and neutral groups is the first step of most physicochemical questions, because ionization at physiological pH follows from it and absorption follows from that. The neutral list covers hydrocarbons, ethers, alcohols, and the carbonyl family of ketones, aldehydes, esters, amides, ureas, carbonates and carbamates. Acidic covers carboxylic acids, phenols, sulfonamides and imides. Basic covers amines, with aliphatic stronger than aryl. Enalapril is the deck\'s worked example, carrying an amide, an ester, a carboxylic acid and a secondary amine at once, so a single molecule can hold all three categories.',
 teachImg:'pp_neutral_groups',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 1 (082726_PhysicochemicalProp1.pdf) slides 19–20',
 quote:'Neutral Functional Groups · ketone · esters · aldehyde · amide · urea'},

{id:'pc-4', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'ionization', concept:'ionization-direction-acid',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'An acidic drug moves from the stomach at pH 2 to plasma at pH 7.4. What happens to its ionization?',
 options:[
  {t:'Ionization increases, because raising pH favours the conjugate base', correct:true,
   why:'Raising the pH removes protons from the surroundings, so the equilibrium shifts toward the deprotonated form. For amobarbital at pKa 8.0 the deck works this through: 100 percent unionized at pH 2.0, 80 percent unionized at pH 7.4.'},
  {t:'Ionization decreases, because acids are less ionized at higher pH', correct:false,
   why:'This is the correct relationship inverted. Acids are less ionized in acidic surroundings, which is why an acidic drug is absorbed well from the stomach, existing there in the unionized lipophilic form.'},
  {t:'Ionization is unchanged, since pKa is fixed', correct:false,
   why:'pKa is indeed a constant of the molecule, but ionization depends on the difference between pH and pKa. The same drug is differently ionized in the stomach and in plasma without its pKa changing at all.'},
  {t:'The drug becomes neutral and precipitates', correct:false,
   why:'The direction is backwards, since raising pH makes an acid more charged rather than neutral, and a charged species is more water soluble rather than less.'}],
 teach:'One rule covers both drug types: a molecule is least ionized when the surrounding pH matches its own character, and the unionized lipophilic form is the one preferentially absorbed. An acidic drug is therefore unionized and well absorbed in acidic surroundings, and a basic drug is unionized and well absorbed in basic ones. The deck works both through, with amobarbital at pKa 8.0 moving from 100 percent unionized at pH 2.0 to 50 percent at pH 8.0, and phenylpropanolamine at pKa 9.4 sitting 99 percent ionized at pH 7.4. The same reasoning drives pH trapping in the renal tubule, used there to speed elimination rather than absorption.',
 teachImg:'pp_amobarbital_trend',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 1 (082726_PhysicochemicalProp1.pdf) slides 24, 29–33',
 quote:'General rule: Acidic drugs exists as unionized form (lipophilic) in acidic environment and are absorbed well'},

{id:'pc-5', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'logp', concept:'logp-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What does the partition coefficient measure?',
 options:[
  {t:'How a compound distributes between an organic phase and water at equilibrium', correct:true,
   why:'Partitioning is the movement of molecules from one phase to another, and the coefficient is the ratio at equilibrium. Octanol is used as the organic phase because it resembles biological membranes, and log P is the logarithm of that ratio.'},
  {t:'How fast a compound dissolves in gastric fluid', correct:false,
   why:'That is dissolution rate, a pharmaceutical property tied to the dosage form. Partitioning is an equilibrium position rather than a rate, and it says nothing about how quickly it is reached.'},
  {t:'The fraction of drug bound to plasma protein', correct:false,
   why:'Protein binding is a distribution property measured separately. Lipophilicity does influence it, but the two are different quantities.'},
  {t:'The proportion of drug that is ionized at a given pH', correct:false,
   why:'That is calculated from pKa and pH through the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation. Ionization and partitioning are connected, since the ionized form partitions poorly into an organic phase, but they are distinct measurements.'}],
 teach:'Log P puts lipophilicity on a single scale, which is what lets it predict behaviour across absorption, distribution and route of administration. The value can also be estimated rather than measured, by adding fragment contributions: aliphatic carbon +0.5, phenyl +2.0, hydroxyl or ether -1.0, carboxyl or ester -0.7, amine -1.0. Summing those gives the calculated value, ClogP, which is how ibuprofen reaches +4.3 from six aliphatic carbons, one phenyl and one carboxyl. Reading a structure as a sum of fragments is what turns a drawing into a prediction about membranes.',
 teachImg:'pp_partition',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 3 (090326_PhysicochemicalProp3.pdf) slides 12–16',
 quote:'What is partitioning? The movement of molecules from one phase to another · Drugs partition between the aqueous phase and lipophilic membrane'},

{id:'pc-6', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'logp', concept:'hydrophilic-group-effect',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A hydroxyl group is added to a drug. What happens to log P and to water solubility?',
 options:[
  {t:'Log P falls and water solubility rises', correct:true,
   why:'A hydroxyl both donates and accepts hydrogen bonds, and water solubility is roughly proportional to the number of groups able to do so. Its fragment contribution to log P is -1.0, so adding one moves the molecule toward the aqueous phase on both measures.'},
  {t:'Log P rises and water solubility falls', correct:false,
   why:'That is what adding a lipophilic fragment does, such as an alkyl chain at +0.5 per carbon or a phenyl ring at +2.0. Hydroxyl moves the molecule the other way.'},
  {t:'Both log P and water solubility rise', correct:false,
   why:'The two move oppositely by construction, since log P measures preference for the organic phase over water. A change that improves aqueous solubility necessarily lowers that preference.'},
  {t:'Neither changes, because log P depends only on molecular weight', correct:false,
   why:'Log P depends on which functional groups are present rather than on size. Two molecules of equal weight can differ widely in log P according to how polar their groups are.'}],
 teach:'Adding a polar group is one of the two named approaches to a solubility problem, the other being salt formation, and both work by increasing interaction with water. Hydroxyls and amino groups matter most because they both donate and accept hydrogen bonds. There is one exception: an intramolecular hydrogen bond, where the donor and acceptor are close enough to bond to each other rather than to water, decreases water solubility and increases lipid solubility, with salicylic acid as the example. The reverse approach addresses a lipophilicity problem, by adding benzene groups or alkyl chains or masking a polar group.',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 2 (090226_PhysicochemicalProp2.pdf) slides 6, 9, 12–13',
 quote:'Generally, water solubility of the compound is proportional to the sum of each FG capable of donating or accepting a hydrogen bond.'},

{id:'pc-7', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'logp', concept:'lipophilic-group-effect',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Two analogues differ only in that one carries an extra ethyl group. How do they compare?',
 options:[
  {t:'The ethyl analogue has the higher log P and the lower water solubility', correct:true,
   why:'An ethyl group is two aliphatic carbons, contributing about +0.5 each, so log P rises by roughly 1.0. Hydrocarbon fragments cannot form hydrogen bonds, so they add to the hydrophobic character of the molecule and reduce its water solubility.'},
  {t:'The ethyl analogue has the lower log P and the higher water solubility', correct:false,
   why:'That is what adding a polar group does, such as a hydroxyl at -1.0. Alkyl chains work in the opposite direction, which is why they are used deliberately to address a lipophilicity problem.'},
  {t:'Both have identical log P since the core is unchanged', correct:false,
   why:'Log P is the sum of all fragment contributions, so any added fragment changes it. Enalapril and enalaprilat differ by one ethyl ester and their calculated values differ, 1.77 against 1.17.'},
  {t:'The ethyl analogue is more ionized at physiological pH', correct:false,
   why:'An ethyl group has no acidic or basic character, so it does not change ionization. It shifts lipophilicity, which is a separate property from ionization even though both affect absorption.'}],
 teach:'Adding lipophilic bulk is a deliberate design move with predictable consequences, and it has three uses here. Enalapril is the ethyl ester of enalaprilat, raising ClogP from 1.17 to 1.77 and oral bioavailability from 30 to 40 percent up to 50 to 70 percent. Haloperidol decanoate raises ClogP from 3.49 to 7.3, which makes it take up into fatty tissue and release slowly, so it can be given by intramuscular injection once a month. Propranolol at 2.65 penetrates the blood-brain barrier while atenolol at 0.5 does not, which is why one has central side effects and the other does not.',
 teachImg:'pp_pi_fragments',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 3 (090326_PhysicochemicalProp3.pdf) slides 15, 20–23',
 quote:'C (aliphatic) +0.5 · Phenyl +2.0 · O (hydroxyl, phenol, ether) –1.0'},

{id:'pc-8', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'logp', concept:'logp-membrane-tradeoff',
 tags:['reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Why is an extremely high log P a problem for an oral drug?',
 options:[
  {t:'The drug crosses membranes readily but dissolves poorly in aqueous GI fluid, so little is available to absorb', correct:true,
   why:'Absorption needs both properties at once: the drug must dissolve in the aqueous contents of the gut and then cross a lipid membrane. A very lipophilic molecule is good at the second step and poor at the first, so the amount available to cross is the limit.'},
  {t:'It cannot cross membranes at all', correct:false,
   why:'That is the opposite failure, belonging to a drug that is too hydrophilic: highly water soluble and unable to cross biological membranes. Both extremes fail, which is why a balance is required.'},
  {t:'It is always ionized in the gut', correct:false,
   why:'Ionization depends on pKa and pH rather than on lipophilicity, and a very lipophilic drug is typically a poorly ionizable one. Ionization and partitioning are separate properties that both affect absorption.'},
  {t:'It is destroyed by gastric acid', correct:false,
   why:'Chemical stability in the gastrointestinal tract is a separate requirement for an oral drug. It is unrelated to log P, since stability depends on which bonds are present rather than on overall polarity.'}],
 teach:'The ideal sits between two failures. Too hydrophilic gives good water solubility and poor membrane penetration. Too lipophilic gives good membrane penetration and poor water solubility, along with distribution into high-fat organs. What is needed is a balance between aqueous solubility and the ability to cross biological membranes. That balance also explains what metabolism is doing, since Phase 1 and Phase 2 reactions lower log P deliberately to make a drug eliminable, and it explains why an extremely lipophilic drug such as amiodarone at cLogP 9 accumulates in tissue and clears slowly.',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 2 (090226_PhysicochemicalProp2.pdf) slides 4–5',
 quote:'Too lipophilic (nonpolar) · Good membrane penetration · Poor water solubility · A balance between hydrophilicity (aqueous solubility) & lipophilicity (ability to cross biological membranes)'},

{id:'ad-1', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'abc-vs-slc',
 tags:['definition','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'How do ABC and SLC transporters differ?',
 options:[
  {t:'ABC transporters are primary active efflux pumps using ATP; SLC transporters are secondary active and generally facilitate influx', correct:true,
   why:'The energy source separates them. ABC proteins hydrolyse ATP directly, which is what primary active means. SLC proteins ride a gradient something else already built, such as the proton gradient used by PEPT1 and MCT1, which is what secondary active means.'},
  {t:'ABC transporters facilitate influx; SLC transporters are efflux pumps', correct:false,
   why:'The two families are swapped. ABC is the efflux family containing P-glycoprotein, MRPs and BCRP, and every ABC transporter in this deck is an efflux pump. SLC does include some transporters capable of efflux, but the family is characterised as generally carrying drug inward.'},
  {t:'Both use ATP directly', correct:false,
   why:'Only ABC is primary active. Secondary active transport depends on a gradient that a primary active pump created elsewhere, so ATP is spent but not by the SLC transporter itself.'},
  {t:'Neither has a role in drug disposition', correct:false,
   why:'Many members of both families are central to drug disposition, and the two named intestinal examples run in opposite directions: P-glycoprotein limits bioavailability by pumping drug back into the lumen, and PEPT1 is deliberately used as a delivery route.'}],
 teach:'Two questions settle any transporter in this deck: where the energy comes from, and which way the drug moves. ABC transporters hydrolyse ATP directly and sit largely on apical membranes pumping drug out, so they oppose absorption. SLC transporters use an existing gradient and mostly carry drug in, so they assist it. The consequence follows from the direction rather than from the family name: high expression of efflux transporters limits bioavailability, while high expression of influx transporters can be exploited as a delivery mechanism. The four with the highest intestinal expression are P-glycoprotein and BCRP from the efflux side, and PEPT1 and MCT1 from the influx side.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 14–15, 18–25',
 quote:'Primary active transporters: Most commonly use adenosine triphosphate (ATP) as an energy source · Example: ABC transporters (ATP-binding cassette) family'},

{id:'ad-2', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'pgp-identity-substrates',
 tags:['recall','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'P-glycoprotein belongs to which transporter family, and what are its substrates?',
 options:[
  {t:'MDR1 within the ABC family; loperamide, digoxin, fexofenadine and paclitaxel', correct:true,
   why:'P-glycoprotein is the common name for MDR1, gene ABCB1. It sits on the apical membrane in most tissues, and its substrates are generally lipophilic, which means they could cross passively were the pump not returning them.'},
  {t:'OATP within the SLC family; cephalexin and enalapril', correct:false,
   why:'Those two drugs are real transporter substrates but belong to PEPT1, the peptide transporter, because both are peptide-like: cephalexin is a beta-lactam and enalapril an ACE inhibitor. PEPT1 carries drug inward, the opposite of P-glycoprotein.'},
  {t:'MCT1 within the SLC family; lactate and pyruvate', correct:false,
   why:'MCT1 is a monocarboxylate transporter driven by the proton gradient, and lactate and pyruvate are its natural substrates. Pravastatin is its named drug substrate, recognised through its structural similarity to mevalonic acid.'},
  {t:'BCRP within the SLC family; levodopa', correct:false,
   why:'Both halves are misplaced. BCRP is in the ABC family, gene ABCG2, and is an efflux transporter. Levodopa crosses the blood-brain barrier on the L-type amino acid transporter LAT1, an influx carrier.'}],
 teach:'P-glycoprotein is the transporter that recurs across every part of this course, so its details carry weight. It is MDR1 in the ABC family, expressed in intestine, liver, kidney, brain, placenta and tumour cells, and its overexpression in tumour cells is a cause of chemotherapeutic resistance. Its substrates are rarely specific and generally lipophilic. The three lists to keep apart are substrates, loperamide, digoxin, fexofenadine and paclitaxel; inhibitors, verapamil and quinidine; and inducers, rifampin and St John\'s wort. Which list a drug is on decides the direction of the interaction, and the same three lists reappear at the blood-brain barrier.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 21–24',
 quote:'PgP substrates: Loperamide (Imodium), digoxin (Lanoxin), Fexofenadine HCl (Allegra), Paclitaxel (Taxol) etc.'},

{id:'ad-3', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'efflux-effect-on-bioavailability',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A drug is a substrate for an intestinal efflux transporter. What is the effect on its oral bioavailability, and what happens if that transporter is inhibited?',
 options:[
  {t:'Efflux limits absorption and lowers bioavailability; inhibiting the transporter raises bioavailability', correct:true,
   why:'Apical efflux pumps return drug to the gut lumen after it has entered the enterocyte, so a fraction of each dose never reaches the portal circulation. Blocking the pump leaves that fraction absorbed, which is the basis of the rosuvastatin and ritonavir interaction through BCRP.'},
  {t:'Efflux raises bioavailability; inhibiting it lowers bioavailability', correct:false,
   why:'Both halves are inverted. Efflux works against absorption by definition, since it moves drug out of the cell. The answer would fit an influx transporter such as PEPT1, where inhibition would indeed lower absorption.'},
  {t:'Efflux has no effect on bioavailability', correct:false,
   why:'Intestinal efflux by P-glycoprotein is named as a contributor to the low bioavailability of several compounds, and many intestinal drug interactions involve it.'},
  {t:'Efflux converts the drug into an inactive metabolite', correct:false,
   why:'Transporters move molecules without changing them. Chemical conversion in the gut wall is first-pass metabolism, a separate mechanism that reduces bioavailability alongside efflux rather than through it.'}],
 teach:'Efflux and metabolism both reduce bioavailability and both act in the enterocyte, which is why they are drawn together in the transport-metabolism interplay figure. They are distinguishable by what they do to the molecule: efflux returns it unchanged to the lumen, metabolism changes it. The practical signature of an efflux interaction is a change in exposure with no new metabolite and no CYP explanation. Rosuvastatin with ritonavir is the deck\'s example, where inhibiting BCRP raises rosuvastatin exposure. The same logic explains why a P-glycoprotein inducer such as rifampin lowers digoxin levels.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 16, 34–35',
 quote:'Inhibition of transport resulting in increased bioavailability of the transporter substrate.'},

{id:'ad-4', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'pept1-prodrug',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Acyclovir has poor oral bioavailability, but valacyclovir does not. Why?',
 options:[
  {t:'Acyclovir is not a PEPT1 substrate, while the valine ester valacyclovir is recognized by PEPT1 and actively taken up', correct:true,
   why:'Attaching valine makes the molecule peptide-like, so PEPT1 carries it across by proton symport. The ester is then cleaved to release acyclovir. Oral bioavailability rises from 15 to 21 percent for acyclovir to 54 percent for valacyclovir.'},
  {t:'Valacyclovir is effluxed by P-glycoprotein while acyclovir is not', correct:false,
   why:'Efflux lowers absorption, so an effluxed valacyclovir would be absorbed worse than acyclovir rather than better, which is the reverse of what happens. P-glycoprotein also handles lipophilic substrates, loperamide, digoxin, fexofenadine and paclitaxel among them, while acyclovir and valacyclovir are both polar, so neither is a candidate for that pump in the first place.'},
  {t:'Valacyclovir is more lipophilic and crosses by passive diffusion alone', correct:false,
   why:'Improving lipophilicity is a genuine prodrug strategy, used for enalapril and oseltamivir in the Absorption II deck. It is not the one here: valacyclovir works by being recognised by a carrier, which is a different design route.'},
  {t:'Acyclovir is destroyed by gastric acid', correct:false,
   why:'Chemical stability in the gastrointestinal tract is a separate requirement for an oral drug. Acyclovir is absorbed poorly rather than destroyed, and the stated limitation is that it is not a PEPT1 substrate.'}],
 teach:'Influx transporters can be designed for, which is what makes PEPT1 the most useful one in this deck. Its high intestinal expression and tolerance for peptide-like structures have made it a target for oral drug delivery, and the strategy is to attach an amino acid so the carrier recognises the molecule. Valacyclovir is the worked example: the valine ester of acyclovir, carried by PEPT1, cleaved afterwards to release the parent. The same reasoning identifies the other PEPT1 drugs, since captopril, enalapril and cephalexin are all peptide-like. Efflux transporters are designed around instead, by avoiding recognition.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 28–30, 36',
 quote:'Valacyclovir(Valtrex) · PepT1 substrate · Increased oral bioavailability (54%)'},

{id:'ad-5', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'pept1-substrate-classes',
 tags:['recall','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Which drug classes are transported by PEPT1?',
 options:[
  {t:'Beta-lactam antibiotics such as cephalexin and ACE inhibitors such as enalapril', correct:true,
   why:'PEPT1 carries di- and tripeptides by proton symport, so it accepts drugs whose structure resembles a short peptide. Both classes contain an amide bond in a peptide-like arrangement, which is the structural clue that a molecule may use this route.'},
  {t:'Anthracyclines and taxanes', correct:false,
   why:'Paclitaxel is named as a P-glycoprotein substrate, so a taxane is handled by efflux rather than peptide uptake. Neither class is peptide-like.'},
  {t:'Inhaled anesthetics', correct:false,
   why:'These are small volatile molecules that cross membranes by passive diffusion and need no carrier at all. The error is treating efficient entry into the body as evidence that a transporter is doing the work. A carrier has a structural requirement, and PEPT1\u2019s is a peptide-like arrangement; a molecule that crosses freely on its own never engages one, so speed of absorption says nothing about which route was used.'},
  {t:'Nucleoside analogues in their parent form', correct:false,
   why:'Nucleosides and their analogues use nucleoside transporters. Acyclovir is the specific case: it is not a PEPT1 substrate until a valine ester is attached to make valacyclovir.'}],
 teach:'Recognising which transporter handles a drug is usually a matter of reading its structure against the carrier\'s natural substrate. PEPT1 evolved to absorb dietary di- and tripeptides, so peptide-like drugs are recognised, which covers the beta-lactams and the ACE inhibitors, and captopril, enalapril, cephalexin and valacyclovir are the named examples. MCT1 carries monocarboxylates such as lactate and pyruvate, and pravastatin is transported by it through its structural similarity to mevalonic acid. Nucleoside transporters take purines, pyrimidines and their analogues. The natural substrate predicts the drug substrate.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 28–29',
 quote:'Drug substrates: Captopril, enalapril, cephalexin, valacyclovir'},

{id:'ad-6', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'trans', concept:'passive-vs-active-transport',
 tags:['definition','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What distinguishes active transport from facilitated transport?',
 options:[
  {t:'Active transport moves drug against its concentration gradient and requires energy; facilitated transport uses a carrier but only moves drug down its gradient', correct:true,
   why:'Both use a protein carrier. The dividing line is energy: facilitative transporters move molecules only down their concentration gradient, without a separate energy source, while active transport spends energy to move drug from low to high concentration.'},
  {t:'Active transport requires no carrier protein', correct:false,
   why:'Active transport depends entirely on transporter proteins, and most drug transporters are active. The process needing no carrier is passive diffusion, which is how most drugs cross membranes.'},
  {t:'Facilitated transport works against the gradient', correct:false,
   why:'Facilitative transporters cannot, because nothing supplies the energy that would require. Vitamin B12 crossing the gastrointestinal membrane is the deck\'s example of facilitated transport.'},
  {t:'Passive diffusion needs a carrier protein, and facilitated transport does not', correct:false,
   why:'Reversed. Passive diffusion is the simplest way across a membrane and depends only on a concentration gradient, slide 11; facilitated transport is the one that uses a special carrier or transporter molecule, slide 13.'}],
 teach:'Three mechanisms are separated by two questions. Is a carrier involved? Passive diffusion uses none and depends on the gradient, lipophilicity, ionization state, and the area and duration of contact, which is what Fick\'s law expresses. Facilitated transport and active transport both use carriers. Is energy spent? Only active transport can then move drug against its gradient, and it divides again into primary, using ATP directly, and secondary, using a gradient another pump created. Most drugs are absorbed by passive diffusion; most drug transporters are active.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 10–15',
 quote:'Facilitative transporters only move molecules down their concentration gradient · Remember! without the use of a separate energy source'},

{id:'ad-7', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'ddi', concept:'first-pass-definition',
 tags:['definition'], source:'slide',
 stem:'The first-pass effect occurs at which sites?',
 options:[
  {t:'The intestinal epithelium and the liver, before the drug reaches systemic circulation', correct:true,
   why:'An oral dose crosses the intestinal epithelium, enters the portal venous system and passes through the liver before any of it reaches the systemic circulation. Both stages can remove drug, which is why the effect is also called presystemic elimination.'},
  {t:'The kidney and the bladder', correct:false,
   why:'Those are elimination sites, and a drug reaches them only after it has entered the systemic circulation. First pass is defined by happening before that point.'},
  {t:'The lungs only', correct:false,
   why:'Pulmonary metabolism exists, but an oral dose does not reach the lungs until after the liver and the systemic circulation, so the lungs are not part of the first pass.'},
  {t:'The stomach only, through acid degradation', correct:false,
   why:'Acid degradation in the gastrointestinal tract is a real disadvantage of the oral route and is listed alongside first-pass metabolism, but it is chemical rather than enzymatic and happens before absorption rather than during it.'}],
 teach:'First-pass metabolism is one of the three stated disadvantages of the oral route, together with degradation in the gastrointestinal tract and slow onset of action, and it is the one that sets the dose. Because the portal vein delivers everything absorbed from the gut straight to the liver, a drug is exposed twice before it reaches its site of action: once in the enterocyte, where CYP3A4 and efflux transporters act together, and once in the liver. That is why an oral dose can be many times the intravenous dose for the same effect, and why avoiding the first pass is a reason to choose another route.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 5, 8–9',
 quote:'First-pass elimination or presystemic elimination: Elimination in the intestine and liver reduces the amount of drug delivered to the systemic circulation'},

{id:'ad-8', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'ddi', concept:'poor-bioavailability-causes',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'both',
 stem:'Which combination of factors would most plausibly explain poor oral bioavailability?',
 options:[
  {t:'Extensive first-pass metabolism, efflux by intestinal transporters, and poor aqueous solubility', correct:true,
   why:'Each acts at a different stage of the same journey. Poor solubility means the drug never dissolves to be absorbed, efflux returns what did enter the enterocyte, and first-pass metabolism removes part of what survives, in the gut wall and then the liver.'},
  {t:'High expression of intestinal influx transporters', correct:false,
   why:'Influx transporters assist absorption, which is why their high expression is exploited as a drug delivery mechanism. This would raise bioavailability rather than lower it.'},
  {t:'Low plasma protein binding', correct:false,
   why:'Protein binding is a distribution property that acts after absorption, governing how much free drug circulates and how much reaches tissue. It does not change how much of the dose crossed the gut wall.'},
  {t:'A large volume of distribution', correct:false,
   why:'Volume of distribution describes where a drug goes once it is in the circulation, so like protein binding it acts after the question has been settled. A drug can have a large Vd and excellent bioavailability.'}],
 teach:'Bioavailability is the fraction of an oral dose that reaches the systemic circulation, so anything that removes drug before that point lowers it. The deck groups the causes: pharmaceutical, meaning dissolution and disintegration; physicochemical, meaning solubility and lipophilicity; physiological, meaning gastrointestinal pH, metabolism, mucosal thickness and residence time; and interactions with food, other drugs and gut flora. Slide 4 asks the question directly, and the four factors given in the 09/03 lecture and written on that slide are first-pass metabolism, the size and nature of the molecule, transporters, and hydrophilicity or lipophilicity. Distribution properties such as protein binding and volume of distribution sit outside the question entirely.',
 cite:'ADMET Absorption I (090326_ADMETAbsorption_I.pdf) slides 4, 7, 35; the four factors from the 09/03 lecture, written on slide 4',
 quote:'Physicochemical: Solubility and hydrophilicity/lipophilicity · Physiological: GI pH, metabolism, mucosal thickness, and residence time'},

{id:'ad-9', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'ddi', concept:'loperamide-quinidine',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Loperamide is an opioid but normally causes no central effects. Why does adding quinidine change that?',
 options:[
  {t:'P-glycoprotein at the blood–brain barrier keeps loperamide out of the CNS, and quinidine inhibits it', correct:true,
   why:'Loperamide is a P-glycoprotein substrate, so the pump removes it from the brain as fast as it enters and it acts only peripherally. Quinidine is a potent P-glycoprotein inhibitor, so the pump stops, central exposure rises, and respiratory depression follows.'},
  {t:'Quinidine induces CYP3A4 and produces an active metabolite of loperamide', correct:false,
   why:'Quinidine appears in this course as an inhibitor rather than an inducer, and the mechanism here is transport rather than metabolism. No active metabolite is involved; the parent drug simply reaches a place it normally cannot.'},
  {t:'Quinidine raises gastric pH so more loperamide is absorbed', correct:false,
   why:'The question is about central penetration rather than how much drug was absorbed. Loperamide is already absorbed and acting peripherally, which is why it treats diarrhoea.'},
  {t:'Quinidine displaces loperamide from plasma proteins', correct:false,
   why:'Displacement raises free drug in plasma and is a real interaction class, illustrated by phenytoin and valproic acid in the Distribution deck. It is not what is described here, and it would not by itself carry an opioid past an intact efflux barrier.'}],
 teach:'This case shows that the blood-brain barrier is enforced partly by transport rather than only by membrane properties. Loperamide is lipophilic enough to enter the brain; P-glycoprotein removes it, which is why an opioid can be sold for diarrhoea without central effects. Inhibiting the pump removes a safety feature rather than adding an effect, and the result is respiratory depression. The barrier carries P-glycoprotein, BCRP and MRPs on the efflux side and influx carriers on the other, which is how levodopa enters on the L-type amino acid transporter LAT1. The same barrier is a problem when a drug is needed in the brain, as in central fungal, viral or bacterial infection.',
 teachImg:'ad_bbb_loperamide',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 14–15',
 quote:'Because quinidine inhibits P-glycoprotein mediated efflux in the BBB, resulting in increased exposure of the CNS to loperamide and bringing about the respiratory depression.'},

{id:'ad-10', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'vd-equation-meaning',
 tags:['definition','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Volume of distribution is calculated as the amount of drug in the body divided by plasma concentration. What does the resulting value represent?',
 options:[
  {t:'A hypothetical volume, not a real anatomical space', correct:true,
   why:'It is the volume that would be needed to hold all the drug at the concentration actually measured in plasma. Many drugs give values far beyond any physical body compartment, which is why the deck calls it an apparent volume.'},
  {t:'The actual physical volume of plasma', correct:false,
   why:'Plasma volume is a real and much smaller quantity, around 3 litres. A drug confined to plasma would give a Vd near that figure, which is the low end of the scale rather than what Vd means in general.'},
  {t:'The volume of urine produced per day', correct:false,
   why:'Urine output belongs to elimination and has no part in this calculation, which uses only the amount of drug in the body and the plasma concentration.'},
  {t:'The total volume of the gastrointestinal tract', correct:false,
   why:'Vd is a pharmacokinetic construct describing where drug has gone after absorption, so no measurement of the gastrointestinal tract enters it.'}],
 teach:'Vd is amount in the body divided by plasma concentration, which makes it a ratio rather than a measurement, and the ratio is what carries the information. A drug that stays in the bloodstream keeps plasma concentration high, so the quotient is small. A drug that leaves plasma for tissue leaves little behind to measure, so the quotient is large, and it can exceed any real volume in the body: chloroquine gives about 15,000 litres. That is the sense in which the volume is apparent. Reading Vd as a statement about where the drug went, rather than as a space, is what makes the comparisons in this deck work.',
 teachImg:'ad_vd_definition',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 3–5',
 quote:'Remember! Vd is An apparent volume/ hypothetical volume'},

{id:'ad-11', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'vd-interpretation',
 tags:['reasoning','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What does a low volume of distribution tell you about a drug?',
 options:[
  {t:'The drug largely stays in the bloodstream rather than moving into tissue', correct:true,
   why:'A low Vd means plasma concentration is high relative to the dose, which happens when the drug remains in the vascular compartment. High plasma protein binding and low lipophilicity both produce it, since both keep drug in plasma.'},
  {t:'The drug distributes extensively into fat and tissue', correct:false,
   why:'That is a high Vd, and amiodarone is the deck\'s example at 4620 litres in a 70 kg person. Extensive tissue uptake leaves little in plasma, so the calculated volume becomes large.'},
  {t:'The drug is eliminated rapidly', correct:false,
   why:'Vd describes distribution rather than removal. A drug can sit in the vascular compartment and still be cleared slowly, and chlorpropamide shows it: a Vd of 6.8 litres with a plasma half-life of 36 hours.'},
  {t:'The drug has a long half-life', correct:false,
   why:'Half-life depends on both volume of distribution and clearance, so Vd alone cannot settle it. A large Vd does tend to lengthen half-life, which is why amiodarone persists for 40 to 55 days, but the relationship runs the other way from what this option states.'}],
 teach:'Low and high Vd describe two opposite fates for a dose. Low means the drug stayed within the vascular compartment, which is what happens when it is polar or heavily bound to plasma protein. High means the drug is well distributed through the body or sequestered in a tissue reservoir, which is what happens when it is lipophilic. The three named reservoirs are plasma proteins, adipose tissue for lipophilic compounds, and bone for chelating agents and heavy metals, and a drug sequestered anywhere other than plasma protein has a higher Vd. Comparing the two worked examples, amiodarone at 4620 litres and chlorpropamide at 6.8, shows the full range.',
 teachImg:'ad_vd_high_low',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 5, 10, 18',
 quote:'Drugs with a low volume of distribution are those that stay within the vascular compartment'},

{id:'ad-12', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'vd-factors',
 tags:['recall','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'What factors affect drug distribution?',
 options:[
  {t:'Blood flow, membrane permeability, partition coefficient and drug binding to plasma protein or tissue', correct:true,
   why:'Each acts at a different stage of the same journey. Perfusion delivers drug to the tissue, permeability and partition coefficient decide whether it crosses, and binding decides whether it is held in plasma or kept in the tissue once there. Age, gender, body composition and disease modify all of them.'},
  {t:'Gastric emptying rate and tablet disintegration', correct:false,
   why:'Both are absorption factors from the Absorption I deck, acting before the drug reaches the circulation. Distribution begins once it is there.'},
  {t:'Urinary pH and glomerular filtration rate', correct:false,
   why:'Those govern renal elimination, the stage after distribution. They decide how drug leaves rather than where it goes.'},
  {t:'CYP induction and inhibition', correct:false,
   why:'Those are metabolism factors. Distribution and metabolism do connect through protein binding, since only free drug is available to enzymes, but induction and inhibition act on the enzymes rather than on where the drug sits.'}],
 teach:'The four factors describe a sequence rather than a list. Blood flow determines how much drug arrives, and perfusion differs widely between organs, with the highest rates in brain, kidney, liver and heart. Membrane permeability then decides whether it can leave the capillary, and capillaries in liver and kidney are considerably more permeable than those in the brain. Partition coefficient decides whether a molecule that can cross will, since lipid-soluble drugs cross readily and can pass the blood-brain and placental barriers. Binding decides whether it stays, either held in plasma by protein or sequestered in a tissue reservoir.',
 teachImg:'ad_dist_factors',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 6–9',
 quote:'Factors Affecting Distribution: Blood flow · Membrane Permeability · Partition coefficient of the drug · Drug Binding: Plasma proteins, tissues, bone and teeth'},

{id:'ad-13', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'amiodarone-vd',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Amiodarone has a volume of distribution of about 4620 L in a 70 kg person. What explains such a large value?',
 options:[
  {t:'It is highly lipophilic and is taken up extensively by fat tissue, leaving little in plasma', correct:true,
   why:'Vd is inversely related to plasma concentration, so when almost all of a dose partitions into fat, liver, lungs and skin, the concentration left in plasma is tiny and the calculated volume becomes enormous. The same accumulation gives it a half-life of 40 to 55 days.'},
  {t:'It is highly water soluble and confined to plasma', correct:false,
   why:'That produces the opposite result, a small Vd near plasma volume, which is the chlorpropamide case at 6.8 litres.'},
  {t:'It is bound almost entirely to albumin', correct:false,
   why:'Extensive plasma protein binding keeps drug inside the vascular compartment, which lowers Vd rather than raising it. Warfarin at 99 percent bound is the example of that pattern.'},
  {t:'It is eliminated unchanged by the kidney', correct:false,
   why:'Renal clearance is an elimination property and does not enter the Vd calculation. A drug this lipophilic is also poorly cleared renally, since it is reabsorbed from the tubule rather than excreted.'}],
 teach:'Amiodarone is the extreme case that makes the arithmetic visible: 4620 litres is roughly 66 times the body volume of a 70 kg person, which is only possible because the number is a ratio rather than a space. The mechanism is sequestration, where the drug accumulates in a tissue reservoir and forms a depot that empties slowly, giving the long half-life. The same property explains its pulmonary toxicity in MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf slide 55, where drug and des-ethyl metabolite accumulate in lung tissue. Distribution, half-life and toxicity here are three consequences of one physicochemical property.',
 teachImg:'ad_vd_amiodarone',
 note:'Two decks give amiodarone different lipophilicity figures. MCMet-9_17_2026.pdf slide 55 gives cLogP = 9; ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slide 10 gives Log P: 7.78 beside the structure. A calculated log P and a stated log P are different quantities, which accounts for the gap, and neither changes the point: amiodarone is far enough along the lipophilic scale to be taken up by fat, liver, lungs and skin, which is what produces the 4620 L volume of distribution and the 40 to 55 day half-life.',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slide 10',
 quote:'Amiodarone (Cordarone) · Vd = 4620 L for a 70kg person · Highly lipophilic · Taken up by fat tissue, liver, lungs and skin'},

{id:'ad-14', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'protein-binding-displacement',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Two highly protein-bound drugs are given together. What is the concern?',
 options:[
  {t:'They compete for the same binding sites, so free concentration of one may rise and increase its effect', correct:true,
   why:'Binding sites are limited, so two drugs with affinity for the same protein compete. Only the free form is pharmacologically active and available for metabolism and excretion, so displacing even a small bound fraction can change the free concentration substantially when a drug is normally heavily bound.'},
  {t:'They form a covalent complex that is inactive', correct:false,
   why:'Plasma protein binding is reversible and non-covalent, held by electrostatic interactions, hydrogen bonding and van der Waals forces. That reversibility is what allows bound drug to be released as free concentration falls.'},
  {t:'Both drugs will be excreted unchanged', correct:false,
   why:'Displacement works the other way: it raises the free fraction, and free drug is the form that can be metabolized and excreted. Bound drug is the form that cannot.'},
  {t:'Protein binding increases for both drugs', correct:false,
   why:'Competition for a finite number of sites reduces binding rather than increasing it. That is the whole basis of the interaction.'}],
 teach:'Only the free form is pharmacologically active, so plasma protein binding sets how much of a measured total is actually doing anything. Acidic drugs bind serum albumin and basic drugs bind alpha-1 acid glycoprotein, which is why competition happens between drugs of like character. The risk is concentrated in heavily bound drugs with narrow margins, because the free fraction starts small and a modest displacement is a large proportional change. Phenytoin is the deck\'s example, about 90 percent bound, a narrow therapeutic index agent, and displaced by valproic acid. A total level can look normal while the free fraction is not.',
 teachImg:'ad_protein_binding',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 22, 25',
 quote:'Phenytoin is displaced from its protein binding sites by valproic acid and increasing the fraction of unbound drug'},

{id:'ad-15', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'hypoalbuminemia',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'A patient with hypoalbuminemia is given phenytoin at a standard dose. What is the risk?',
 options:[
  {t:'Less binding capacity means a higher free phenytoin concentration and a risk of toxicity at a normal total level', correct:true,
   why:'Phenytoin is an acidic drug bound to albumin, so less albumin means fewer sites and a larger unbound fraction. The measured total concentration can sit in range while the active free portion is elevated, and phenytoin has a narrow therapeutic index.'},
  {t:'Phenytoin will be under-absorbed and ineffective', correct:false,
   why:'Albumin acts on distribution once the drug is in the circulation, not on absorption from the gut. Nothing about low albumin changes how much of an oral dose crosses the intestinal wall.'},
  {t:'Binding increases, lowering free drug', correct:false,
   why:'The direction is inverted. Hypoalbuminemia means less albumin, so fewer binding sites and a larger unbound share rather than a smaller one. Binding capacity and free concentration always move in opposite directions, so a fall in one is a rise in the other. The reason this one is easy to reverse is that the clinical danger sounds like too much binding, when what makes the patient toxic is too little.'},
  {t:'The drug will be entirely confined to plasma', correct:false,
   why:'Binding to albumin is what holds a drug inside the vascular compartment, so less albumin releases more of it to leave, not less. Confinement to plasma describes the heavily bound state, which is the situation this patient has moved furthest away from. The give-away is that the option and the stem describe opposite amounts of albumin while predicting the same distribution.'}],
 teach:'This is the same mechanism as displacement, arriving by a different route: there displacement takes the sites, here there are fewer sites to begin with. Either way the free fraction rises while the total may not, which is why total concentration is a poor guide for a heavily bound drug in a patient with low albumin. Phenytoin and furosemide are the two named, and furosemide is 91 to 99 percent bound. The clinical consequence follows from the general rule that binding limits distribution beyond plasma and limits metabolism, so removing binding accelerates both while raising the active concentration.',
 teachImg:'ad_binding_extent',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 24, 26',
 quote:'In hypoalbuminemia Binding may be reduced, and high concentrations of free drug may be attained e.g. Phenytoin and Furosemide'},

{id:'ad-16', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'bbb-efflux-and-influx',
 tags:['reasoning','case'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Levodopa reaches the brain while many other polar molecules do not. Why?',
 options:[
  {t:'Influx transporters at the blood–brain barrier carry it across, a route exploited deliberately', correct:true,
   why:'Levodopa is carried by the L-type amino acid transporter LAT1, one of the influx carriers that normally bring essential nutrients such as amino acids and glucose across. Designing a drug to be recognised by such a carrier is a deliberate way past the barrier.'},
  {t:'It is lipophilic enough to diffuse passively', correct:false,
   why:'That route is real and is how benzodiazepines such as diazepam enter, since small lipophilic drugs typically have good central nervous system permeation. Levodopa is polar, so it needs a carrier instead.'},
  {t:'P-glycoprotein actively pumps it into the brain', correct:false,
   why:'P-glycoprotein at the barrier pumps drug out of the brain rather than in, which is how it keeps loperamide peripheral. It sits alongside BCRP and the MRPs on the efflux side.'},
  {t:'The blood–brain barrier has no transporters', correct:false,
   why:'It carries both efflux and influx transporters, and that combination is exactly how it selects what reaches the brain: nutrients and designed substrates in, most xenobiotics out.'}],
 teach:'The barrier works in two directions at once, and both are exploitable. Efflux transporters, P-glycoprotein, BCRP and the MRPs, remove drug that entered, which protects the central nervous system and explains why loperamide causes no central opioid effects. Influx transporters bring in nutrients that are too polar to diffuse, and a drug shaped like one of those nutrients can ride the same carrier, which is what levodopa does on LAT1. The barrier is therefore protective in one case and an obstacle in another, and treating severe fungal, viral or bacterial infection of the brain is where it becomes the problem.',
 teachImg:'ad_bbb_transport',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 11–13',
 quote:'Levodopa used Parkinson disease is transported into the brain by the l-type amino acid transporter (LAT) 1'},

{id:'ad-17', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'admet', sub:'vd', concept:'tetracycline-chelation',
 tags:['case','reasoning'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Why are tetracyclines traditionally contraindicated in young children?',
 options:[
  {t:'They chelate divalent cations and deposit in developing bone and teeth', correct:true,
   why:'The acidic functions of the tetracyclines form chelates with calcium and other divalent cations, and bone and teeth are calcium-rich structures being actively formed in a child. The drug accumulates there in proportion to dose and duration, and because tetracyclines are yellow the result is permanent discoloration.'},
  {t:'They are rapidly effluxed by P-glycoprotein', correct:false,
   why:'Efflux limits tissue exposure, so it would work against deposition rather than causing it. The mechanism here is chemical affinity for the tissue, not transport.'},
  {t:'They cross the blood–brain barrier and cause neurotoxicity', correct:false,
   why:'The sequestration site named is bone and teeth rather than the brain. Bone is listed as a reservoir specifically for chelating agents and heavy metals such as lead and radium.'},
  {t:'They are metabolized to a reactive epoxide', correct:false,
   why:'No bioactivation is involved. The parent drug itself binds calcium, which is why the effect depends on how much is given and for how long rather than on any metabolic step.'}],
 teach:'Sequestration means a drug accumulating in a reservoir, and the site follows from the chemistry rather than from the dose. Lipophilic compounds partition into adipose tissue, which is what happens with DDT and similar environmental contaminants and matters in obese patients. Chelating agents and heavy metals go to bone, tetracyclines through calcium binding and lead and radium by adsorption onto the bone surface. Plasma proteins are the third reservoir. A drug sequestered anywhere other than plasma protein typically shows a higher volume of distribution, so the tetracycline case connects the chemistry directly to the pharmacokinetics.',
 teachImg:'ad_sequestration',
 cite:'ADMET Distribution (090426_Distribution.pdf) slides 16–18',
 quote:'Tetracyclines are traditionally contraindicated in children due to accumulation in teeth and staining'},

{id:'pc-9', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'logp', concept:'clogp-fragment-sum',
 tags:['calculation','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Ibuprofen carries one phenyl ring, six aliphatic carbons and one carboxyl group. Using the fragment values π = +0.5 for aliphatic carbon, +2.0 for phenyl and −0.7 for O=C–O, what is its ClogP?',
 options:[
  {t:'+4.3', correct:true,
   why:'Each fragment contributes its own π value and the contributions add: six aliphatic carbons give 6 × 0.5 = +3.0, the phenyl ring gives +2.0, and the carboxyl gives −0.7, so 3.0 + 2.0 − 0.7 = +4.3.'},
  {t:'+5.0', correct:false,
   why:'That is 3.0 + 2.0, the two hydrocarbon fragments summed with the carboxyl left out. The carboxyl is the one polar fragment in ibuprofen and the only negative term in the sum, so dropping it is what separates this answer from the right one.'},
  {t:'+3.6', correct:false,
   why:'That is 3.0 + 2.0 − 0.7 − 0.7, the carboxyl subtracted twice. O=C–O is a single fragment covering the whole carboxyl, so its oxygens are not counted again as separate O fragments at −1.0 or −0.7.'},
  {t:'+2.3', correct:false,
   why:'That is 3.0 − 0.7, the phenyl ring left out. Phenyl at +2.0 is the largest single term in ibuprofen, so omitting it moves the answer further than any other single slip.'}],
 teach:'ClogP, the calculated logarithm of the partition coefficient, is built by adding a π value for every fragment in the structure rather than measured. π is the hydrophilic-lipophilic constant for one fragment: positive fragments push the molecule into the lipid phase and negative fragments hold it in water. Hydrocarbon fragments are positive because they cannot hydrogen bond with water, which is why aliphatic carbon is +0.5 and a phenyl ring is +2.0. Fragments carrying oxygen or nitrogen are negative because they can, which is why O=C–O is −0.7 and a hydroxyl is −1.0. Ibuprofen works out to +4.3 by this method, and the sign of each term tells you which way that fragment pulled: the hydrocarbon fragments supplied +5.0 between them and the single carboxyl pulled 0.7 back.',
 teachImg:'pp_clogp_ibuprofen',
 note:'Count only the carbons that are not already inside another fragment. Ibuprofen has thirteen carbons in total, but the carboxyl carbon belongs to the O=C–O fragment and the six aromatic carbons belong to the phenyl fragment, which leaves the six aliphatic carbons the slide counts.',
 cite:'Physicochemical Properties 3 (090326_PhysicochemicalProp3.pdf) slides 15–17',
 quote:'Fragments π · 6 aliphatic carbons +3.0 (6 x 0.5) · 1 phenyl +2.0 · 1 carboxyl − 0.7 · ClogP +4.3'},

{id:'pc-10', prof:'Yendapally', tier:'old', topic:'physchem', sub:'ionization', concept:'percent-ionization-acid',
 tags:['calculation','exam-pattern'], source:'slide',
 stem:'Aspirin has a pKa of 3.5. At pH 7.0, what percentage of the drug is in the ionized form?',
 options:[
  {t:'99.96%', correct:true,
   why:'Substituting into pKa = pH + log([Unionized]/[Ionized]) gives 3.5 = 7.0 + log([Unionized]/[Ionized]), so log([Unionized]/[Ionized]) = −3.5 and the ratio is 1/3162.27. One unionized molecule per 3162.27 ionized makes the ionized share 3162.27 × 100 / 3163.27 = 99.96%.'},
  {t:'0.03%', correct:false,
   why:'That is the unionized share, 1 × 100 / 3163.27 = 0.03%, which is the other half of the same ratio. The two add to 100%, so the last step is choosing which term goes on top: the question asks for the ionized form, so the 3162.27 belongs in the numerator.'},
  {t:'28.49%', correct:false,
   why:'That is the answer for phenobarbital, whose pKa of 7.4 sits above pH 7.0. There log([Unionized]/[Ionized]) comes out positive at +0.4, so the unionized form dominates and the ionized share is small. Aspirin has a pKa of 3.5, which sits 3.5 units below the pH instead of above it, so the logarithm is negative and the ionized form dominates rather than the unionized one.'},
  {t:'50%', correct:false,
   why:'An acid is half ionized only when pH equals pKa, since log(1) = 0. Here the pH is 3.5 units above the pKa, and every full unit moves the ratio by a factor of ten.'}],
 teach:'The relationship between pKa and pH fixes how much of an acid is charged, and the form used in this course is pKa = pH + log([Unionized]/[Ionized]). An acidic drug donates its proton to become the ionized conjugate base, so a pH above the pKa drives it toward the ionized form and a pH below holds it unionized. The size of the shift follows from the logarithm: each unit of difference between pH and pKa changes the ratio tenfold, so aspirin at 3.5 units below the pH is ionized by a factor of about 3000 to 1. The same substitution run on the other practice drugs gives indomethacin at pKa 4.5 as 99.68% ionized, ibuprofen at pKa 5.2 as 98.43%, and phenobarbital at pKa 7.4 as 28.49%. The further an acid\'s pKa falls below the pH, the more completely it is charged, and a charged drug does not cross a membrane.',
 teachImg:'pp_ionization_worked',
 note:'The 28.49% figure for phenobarbital follows from rounding 10^0.4 to 2.51 before the final division, as the answer key does. Carrying the unrounded 2.5119 through gives 28.47%. Both are the same calculation; the difference is only where the rounding is applied.',
 cite:'Practice Problem Answers (082726_PracticeProblemAnswers.pdf) slides 1–5; setup in Physicochemical Properties 1 (082726_PhysicochemicalProp1.pdf) slides 27, 29–30',
 quote:'3.5 = 7.0 + log [Unionized]/[Ionized] · % Ionized = 3162.27 x 100 / 3163.27 = 99.96 %'}
);
